WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.390
JOE: Understanding how an individual with
autism handles information processing,

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:10.260
is a good way to work out how to find the
right strategies to help the individual.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.790
Once their difficulties are identified
then suitable strategies can be introduced

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.619
to help with those difficulties.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.480
BETH: Other people such as family
members, teachers, employers, or friends

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.510
may need to have a lot of involvement
implementing the strategies, as they may

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:33.080
be used in more than one place; such as
at home, at school or college, or in work.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.319
It is, therefore, important that everyone
involved, uses them consistently.

00:00:38.959 --> 00:00:42.740
Some individuals with autism may
develop enough to be able to use the

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.560
strategies independently however,
some may always need support.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:51.579
JOE: Visual references can be a
good way of illustrating what to

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.770
do and at what time, but they can
also be helpful in showing the

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:01.050
consequences of an action, such as
missing an appointment or a deadline.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.229
Sequence cards, timers and calendars
can help with structuring the day

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.560
at home, in education or work.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.419
BETH: Education and work can cause great
anxiety for everyone when homework,

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.590
assignments and deadlines are involved.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.289
Imagine how much more problematic
this is for individuals with

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.930
autism, who may have little or no
concept of time and consequences.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.409
JOE: They may need extra help with
using strategies to organise their work,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:33.770
using lists, calendars and colour coding
to emphasise importance and timing.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.679
Using different coloured files to
identify and clarify importance, can

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:43.029
help with deadlines and making a list
and ticking off as things are completed

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.490
in sequence, can help to show progress.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.739
BETH: Processing of instructions can
be difficult, so it may be useful

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.860
to use communication books, online
learning environments, and voice

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.020
recordings to reduce the pressure on
the individual of trying to remember

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.410
what they are supposed to be doing.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.739
Computer programmes, digital calendars
and alerts can also be a reminder for

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.910
appointments, deadlines and meetings
and help to keep them on track.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.070
JOE: Like most adults, many individuals
with autism will need to manage their

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.550
own money or bills, to varying extents.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:20.290
Children may have to learn to handle
pocket money, giving them an allowance

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.720
and getting them to save with a savings
account, can help as they get older.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.560
Money management apps could be used
to help show how saving works and the

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.850
the consequences of not spending
wisely should be stressed.

00:02:34.309 --> 00:02:38.409
BETH: Some Individuals with autism
may also need help in understanding

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.880
what is the end goal of what
they are working to, with all the

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.560
continuous work and deadlines.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.630
Having that goal evidenced
visually will help them to see

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.950
this and focus on it going forward.
